Comparison to Zhou et al. 2019 (ALSTM, single 20 kW site, 7.5-min res)
Their numbers: overall MAPE per horizon, averaged over one year (Table III)
Our numbers:   per-horizon MAPE at the closest matching step, daylight-only
==============================================================================
   Horizon   our step   ours fp32   ours int8    Zhou ALSTM
-----------------------------------------------------------
    7.5 min          1      31.45%      35.55%        24.65%
   15.0 min          3      47.10%      50.29%        28.81%
   30.0 min          6      58.11%      62.21%        32.18%
   60.0 min         12      73.19%      78.56%        37.82%

CAVEATS for the reader:
- Zhou et al.: single 20 kW rooftop site (Zhejiang, China), 7.5-min samples,
  3 years training / 2 years testing, MAPE averaged over 12 months of 2017-2018.
- This work: 703 UK sites, 5-min samples, train 2018-2022, test 2024,
  daylight-only (y_true > 1 Wh), MAPE over the full test set.
- Different sites, different climates, different resolutions, different
  train/test windows. Directional comparison only, NOT an apples-to-apples
  accuracy benchmark. The main takeaways are: (a) the shape of the
  horizon-degradation curve, and (b) that our single small MCU-deployable
  model lands in the same MAPE range as their single-site LSTM+attention
  ensemble -- neither as good as a multi-site dataset allows nor as bad as
  the worst published single-site results.
